<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ headline.claim }} — it-job-radar</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ headline.detail | default('Polish IT job market: technology demand and salary ranges, from a bounded, documented sample.') }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ headline.claim }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ headline.detail | default('Polish IT job market analysis.') }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="https://p0w3r223.github.io/it-job-radar/">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
<link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns=%22http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%22 viewBox=%220 0 16 16%22><text y=%2213%22 font-size=%2213%22>&#128225;</text></svg>">
<style>
{{ styles }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <p class="eyebrow">Polish IT job market · snapshot {{ manifest.snapshot.observed_date }}</p>
  <h1>{{ headline.claim }}</h1>
  {% if headline.detail %}
  <p class="lead">{{ headline.detail }}</p>
  {% endif %}
</header>

<ul class="kpis">
  {% for kpi in kpis %}
  <li class="kpi">
    <div class="kpi-value">{{ kpi.value }}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">{{ kpi.label }}</div>
    <div class="kpi-note">{{ kpi.note }}</div>
  </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>

<h2>What a junior offer actually is</h2>
<p>
  Job boards present junior roles as one category. Split by the kind of work the title
  describes, they are not: IT support and administration is the largest single group,
  ahead of every engineering discipline. A ranking of "top technologies for juniors" taken
  without that split describes a job most of its readers are not applying for.
</p>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.junior_roles }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    Offers open to juniors by role family. <strong>n = {{ junior_n }}</strong>.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
{% if junior_n < min_stratum_n %}
<div class="card caution">
  <strong>Read this one carefully.</strong> {{ junior_n }} junior offers is under the
  {{ min_stratum_n }} this page treats as the floor for a quantified claim. It is enough to
  notice which category is largest, not to put a percentage on it.
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>What the market asks for</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.technologies }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    Distinct offers listing each technology as a <em>requirement</em>. Nice-to-haves are
    excluded: pooling them overweights optional skills.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Offers by role family</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.roles }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    Classified from the title by an auditable rule table. Titles matching nothing stay as
    <code>other</code> rather than being forced into a family
    ({{ '%.0f%%' | format(manifest.quality.role_family_other_share * 100) }} of offers).
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>What it pays</h2>
<p>
  B2B is net on invoice, employment is gross. They are different amounts of money for the
  same work, so they are never pooled — and shown side by side rather than as one number.
  Each row is the median lower-to-upper range; the thin line is the bootstrap interval
  around the lower bound. Where it is wide, the figure is not reliable.
</p>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.salary_b2b }}</div>
  <figcaption>B2B contracts, PLN per month, net.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.salary_employment }}</div>
  <figcaption>Employment contracts, PLN per month, gross.</figcaption>
</figure>
{% if thin_strata %}
<p class="note">
  Greyed rows rest on fewer than {{ min_stratum_n }} offers:
  {% for level, count in thin_strata.items() %}{{ level }} ({{ count }}){% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}.
  They are shown rather than dropped, because a silently missing level is harder to notice
  than a visibly uncertain one.
</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>Where the work is</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.work_modes }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    An offer can list several modes, so these deliberately sum to more than the offer
    count — which is why this is not a pie chart.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>How much of the market this covers</h2>
<table>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th>Offers listed by the source today</th><td class="num">{{ manifest.coverage.offers_listed }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Offers whose attributes we hold</th><td class="num">{{ manifest.coverage.attributes_known }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Coverage</th><td class="num">{{ '%.1f%%' | format(manifest.coverage.share * 100) }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Offers disclosing a salary</th><td class="num">{{ '%.0f%%' | format(manifest.quality.salary_disclosure_rate * 100) }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Snapshot age</th><td class="num">{{ manifest.quality.freshness_days | int }} days</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<figure>
  <div class="chart-wrap">{{ charts.coverage }}</div>
  <figcaption>
    One slot per recorded run, not per day — runs sit minutes or days apart, and spacing
    them along a date axis would draw a rate nobody measured. A filled marker is a run that
    spent its page budget; a hollow one only re-read the sitemap, which costs a handful of
    requests and moves the frame rather than the coverage. The scale is the accumulation's
    own, not the market's: against {{ manifest.coverage.offers_listed }} listed offers this
    whole climb is a flat line, so the distance still to go is written on the chart rather
    than drawn — the share on the final point is the honest reading of it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<p class="note">
  Presence is complete — one sitemap request identifies every live offer — while
  attributes are fetched at most once per offer under a bounded daily budget. Coverage
  therefore grows over time without ever increasing load on the source. This is the
  evidence for that claim rather than the claim itself: {{ runs_recorded }} runs so far.
</p>

<h2>Method, and what it cannot tell you</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p>
    <strong>Collection.</strong> A bounded, documented sample of
    <a href="{{ manifest.source.url }}">{{ manifest.source.name }}</a>: throttled, no
    personal data, attribution kept. Never the whole base.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Normalization.</strong> Technology names are unified before counting
    (<code>ReactJS</code> and <code>React.js</code> are one thing), seniority follows the
    Polish convention where <em>regular</em> means mid, and hourly rates are scaled to a
    monthly equivalent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Limits.</strong> One source, so this is that board's market, not the whole
    market. Salaries only describe offers that disclose one — a minority. Offers already
    listed when observation began have an unknown start date, so they cannot support any
    claim about how long a posting stays open. Currencies are not converted.
  </p>
</div>

<h2>The queries behind every figure</h2>
<p class="note">
  Each metric is defined once, as SQL, and this is that file — not a description of it.
</p>
{% for name, sql in queries.items() %}
<details>
  <summary>{{ name }}.sql</summary>
  <pre><code>{{ sql }}</code></pre>
</details>
{% endfor %}

<footer>
  <p>
    Built {{ generated[:10] }} from snapshot
    <time datetime="{{ manifest.snapshot.observed_date }}">{{ manifest.snapshot.observed_date }}</time>,
    commit <code>{{ manifest.git_sha }}</code> ·
    <a href="data/manifest.json">manifest</a> ·
    <a href="https://github.com/P0w3r223/it-job-radar">source on GitHub</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    The published dataset is derived and redacted: no titles, no employer names, no offer
    URLs. It answers questions about the market and deliberately cannot answer questions
    about individual listings.
  </p>
  <p>{{ manifest.source.attribution }}</p>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
